{
  id:'npqb-nr-159', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'Complete loss of consciousness occurs in all of the following EXCEPT:',
  options:['Atonic seizures','Tonic seizures','Myoclonic seizures','Generalized tonic-clonic seizures'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Myoclonic seizures are the exception the bank keys — consciousness is classically preserved during the brief jerk.** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` states the rule directly: *"ALL generalized seizures involve COMPLETE loss of consciousness — EXCEPT myoclonic (brief) and atonic"*, and separately, *"MYOCLONIC ... Brief, sudden muscle jerks. Consciousness relatively preserved!"* The deck also flags myoclonic seizures as *"Worst in morning. JME hallmark"* — the same seizure type tested by name in the juvenile myoclonic epilepsy vignette of `npqb-nr-175`.\n\n**Why brevity is the mechanism, not just the label.** *(This physiological reasoning is not taken from the course material.)* Loss of consciousness in a generalized seizure requires sustained, bilateral disruption of the thalamocortical arousal circuits — long enough for the reticular activating system to be overwhelmed. A myoclonic jerk lasts a fraction of a second, too brief for that disruption to register as unawareness even though the discharge itself is generalized; the patient often reports the jerk immediately afterward, which would be impossible with true loss of consciousness.\n\n**Tonic** seizures produce sudden bilateral stiffening with falls (the deck: *"Sudden sustained stiffening. Bilateral. Falls."*), and **generalized tonic-clonic** seizures are the most dramatic generalized type, with a tonic phase, a fall, clonic jerking and post-ictal confusion (*"Most dramatic. Tonic phase → fall → clonic jerks. Postictal confusion. Duration 1-3 min."*) — both last long enough for the arousal circuits to be shut down throughout, and both carry complete loss of consciousness without dispute.\n\n**Why atonic belongs in the same exception, clinically.** The deck describes atonic (drop attack) seizures as *"Sudden loss of muscle tone → fall. Injury risk. Helmet essential"* and groups them with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome — the fall is a passive collapse from lost tone, not a sustained convulsion, and like the myoclonic jerk it is over in an instant. Nothing about the mechanism of an atonic seizure implies a longer disruption of arousal than a myoclonic one; the bank\'s choice to key only myoclonic here is a printed decision, not a mechanistic distinction this material supports.\n\n**⚠️ A note on this key, recorded rather than disputed.** The same slide that supplies the rule names *two* exceptions to complete loss of consciousness — myoclonic **and atonic** — yet atonic seizures are also offered here as a separate option and are not the one keyed. The bank\'s printed key selects only myoclonic. This is held as printed; the discrepancy between the source lecture\'s own two-exception rule and the bank\'s single-exception key is noted here, not resolved by changing the answer.\n\n**Bottom line:** as printed, myoclonic seizures are the exception with preserved consciousness because the discharge is too brief to disrupt arousal circuits; the source lecture would also exempt atonic seizures on the same mechanistic grounds, and that tension is recorded rather than adjudicated.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Tonic and generalized tonic-clonic seizures cause complete loss of consciousness; myoclonic seizures classically spare it — though the cached lecture also exempts atonic seizures, a tension this bank\'s key does not resolve.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.33 (answer p.36)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-160', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'Intractable seizures:',
  options:['Can be treated by wide-spectrum antiepileptics','Incurable','Treated surgically','None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The bank prints an explanation box for this question, transcribed here verbatim:**\n\n> Surgical treatment is an option for patients with intractable seizures who have failed multiple medication trials. Surgical options might include resecting the focus of seizures in the brain or implanting devices like a vagus nerve stimulator\n\n`L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` defines the term the box assumes: *"Drug-Resistant Epilepsy = Failure of ≥2 appropriate AEDs at adequate doses"*, and lists the non-pharmacological options beyond that point — **epilepsy surgery** (*"Resection of seizure focus. Most effective for temporal lobe. 70% seizure-free post-op"*, and it *"Requires video-EEG + neuroimaging"* to select candidates), **vagus nerve stimulation** (*"Implanted device. Stimulates left vagus nerve. ~50% reduction in seizure frequency. Non-invasive, no cognitive SE"*), the **ketogenic diet** (*"High fat, low carb. Effective in children. Mechanism: altered brain metabolism. Requires specialist dietitian"*), **deep brain stimulation**, **responsive neurostimulation** and **laser ablation**.\n\n**Why the device-vs-surgery options sit at different points on the same ladder.** Resective surgery removes the seizure focus outright and offers the best odds of a true cure (70% seizure-free) but is invasive and only reaches that figure for a well-localised temporal focus confirmed on video-EEG and imaging. VNS is reversible and carries no cognitive side effect, but its ceiling — a roughly 50% reduction in frequency — is a palliative gain, not a cure; it suits patients who are not surgical candidates or who decline resection. Both share the same entry criterion the box invokes: failure of at least two adequate drug trials first.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:** wide-spectrum antiepileptics are exactly what has already failed by definition of drug resistance, so option a restates the problem as its own solution. "Incurable" is too absolute — surgery renders 70% of selected temporal lobe cases seizure-free, which is a cure in practical terms for many patients. "None of the above" is wrong because surgical and device options are a genuine, named management path.',
  objective:'Drug-resistant (intractable) epilepsy — failure of two or more adequate antiepileptic trials — is managed by resective surgery, neurostimulation devices or the ketogenic diet, not by more of the same drug class.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.33 (answer p.36, explanation box printed)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-161', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'Epilepsy is:',
  options:['Recurrent unprovoked convulsions','Recurrent unprovoked seizures','Recurrent provoked seizures','A single seizure episode'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**"Recurrent unprovoked seizures" is the ILAE definition, and every other option changes one word that breaks it.** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` gives the definition directly: *"Epilepsy (ILAE 2014): A brain disorder characterized by an enduring predisposition to generate seizures and its consequences"*, operationalised as *"At least 2 unprovoked seizures occurring >24 hours apart"* or *"1 unprovoked seizure with a high risk of recurrence (>60%) over 10 years"*.\n\n**Why the >24-hour and >60% clauses matter, not just the word "recurrent."** Two seizures within the same 24 hours (a cluster during one acute illness) are generally treated as one event rather than two independent recurrences — the clause exists to stop a single prolonged or clustered episode from being mislabelled as epilepsy. The single-seizure clause exists for the mirror reason: a patient does not have to wait for a second seizure if the first already carries a high enough recurrence risk *(the specific risk drivers behind that figure, such as a remote lesion or an epileptiform EEG, are not taken from the course material; the >60% threshold itself is)*.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:** *"convulsions"* (option a) is narrower than *"seizures"* — it implies visible motor activity and excludes non-convulsive presentations such as absence seizures, which are still epilepsy. *"Provoked seizures"* (option c) is disqualified outright by the same slide: *"Provoked (Acute Symptomatic): Occur in close temporal relationship with systemic insult (fever, metabolic shift, trauma). These do not constitute epilepsy."* A **single** seizure (option d) does not meet the recurrence requirement unless the >60% ten-year recurrence-risk clause applies, which the bare option does not state.\n\n**Bottom line:** epilepsy = recurrent, *unprovoked* seizures (or one seizure with a high predicted recurrence) — the word "unprovoked" is what separates it from an acute symptomatic event, and "seizures" rather than "convulsions" is what keeps non-motor types inside the definition.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Epilepsy is defined as recurrent unprovoked seizures (or one seizure with a high recurrence risk); provoked (acute symptomatic) events, by definition, do not count.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.33 (answer p.36)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-162', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'A 10 yrs. old male presenting with repeated attacks of clonic movement in the right upper limb and face. Each attack last for 1-2min. During the attack patient is fully conscious and responding to verbal orders. What is the most probable diagnosis?',
  options:['Partial motor seizures','Complex partial seizures','Partial motor seizure secondary generalization','Generalized tonic-clonic seizures','Chorea'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The bank prints an explanation box for this question, transcribed here verbatim:**\n\n> The patient\'s presentation is consistent with focal (partial) seizures affecting the right upper limb and face with clonic movements. During these attacks, the patient remains fully conscious and responsive, which is typical of simple partial seizures (now called focal seizures without impaired awareness).\n\n`L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` supplies the framework the box is using: focal seizures *"Arise from one hemisphere / network"*, and are split by awareness — *"Focal AWARE (Simple Partial): Patient remains fully conscious. Knows what\'s happening. Can recall the event"* — against clonic movement itself listed as a **motor semiology** (*"Clonic (rhythmic jerking)"*). Because the right face and arm sit adjacent to each other on the motor cortex, a single focal discharge over the left precentral gyrus can produce exactly this combined face-plus-arm pattern without the seizure needing to spread further *(the cortical-mapping reasoning is not taken from the course material)*; on EEG this would be expected to show **focal spikes** localised to that same cortex (*"Focal Spikes: Localized epileptogenic cortex"*).\n\n**Why the others are wrong:** **Complex partial seizures** (option b) are the modern *impaired*-awareness focal type — the deck: *"Consciousness is altered ... No recall of event"* — which contradicts a patient answering verbal orders. **Secondary generalisation** (option c) and **generalized tonic-clonic seizures** (option d) both require evolution to a bilateral convulsion with loss of consciousness, which this vignette never describes — the discharge here stays confined to one hemisphere\'s network for the full 1-2 minutes without spreading. **Chorea** (option e) is a continuous, non-paroxysmal involuntary movement disorder rather than a discrete, timed seizure with a defined 1-2 minute attack and full recovery *(the chorea distinction is not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Bottom line:** clonic movement confined to one limb and the face, with preserved consciousness and responsiveness throughout, is a focal aware (simple partial) motor seizure — if it later evolved to involve the whole body with loss of consciousness, this same case would instead become the next distractor, secondary generalisation.',
  objective:'A discrete, timed attack of clonic movement in one limb/face with the patient fully conscious and responsive throughout is a focal aware (simple partial) motor seizure, not a complex partial or generalized event.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.33 (answer p.36, explanation box printed)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-163', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'Which of the following drugs can exacerbate absence seizures?',
  options:['Carbamazepine','Valproate','Ethosuximide','Clonazepam'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Carbamazepine is the drug the deck singles out by name as forbidden in absence seizures.** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` states it as a bare instruction under the absence-seizure treatment column: *"NEVER: Carbamazepine"* (alongside *"NEVER: Phenytoin"* and *"NEVER: Vigabatrin"*), repeats it as a standalone pearl — *"3Hz spike-wave on EEG. Children. DON\'T use carbamazepine!"* — and drives the point home a third time in the deck\'s own worked case: *"CRITICAL: NEVER use Carbamazepine or Phenytoin in absence seizures — they worsen seizures!"*\n\n**Why a drug aimed at focal seizures makes a generalized seizure worse rather than better.** *(This mechanism is not taken from the course material.)* Absence seizures arise from a 3 Hz thalamocortical spike-and-wave rhythm rather than a focal, sodium-channel-driven discharge; carbamazepine and phenytoin (sodium-channel blockers) and vigabatrin (a GABA-transaminase inhibitor) can each paradoxically aggravate generalized spike-wave activity, precipitating more frequent absences or even myoclonic jerks rather than suppressing them — the same two lead drugs recur as forbidden in the juvenile myoclonic epilepsy case of `npqb-nr-175`.\n\n**Why the others are wrong: Valproate and Ethosuximide** are the two first-line drugs for absence seizures on the same slide (*"1st Line: Ethosuximide (first choice), Valproate"*) — genuine treatments, not aggravants. **Clonazepam** is listed as a 1st-line option for myoclonic seizures on the same slide and is not flagged anywhere in the deck as worsening absence seizures.\n\n**Bottom line:** carbamazepine (with phenytoin and vigabatrin) worsens absence seizures because it targets the wrong mechanism for a generalized spike-wave disorder, and must never be used for them; valproate and ethosuximide are the drugs that actually treat them.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Carbamazepine, phenytoin and vigabatrin can all worsen absence seizures and must be avoided; ethosuximide and valproate are the first-line treatments.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.33 (answer p.36). The bank prints the stem as "exarcerbate"; corrected to *exacerbate* here.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-164', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'Febrile convulsions + 3 afebrile seizures, what is the best management?',
  options:['Sodium valproate','Antiepileptic for seizures and antipyretic for febrile convulsions','Two antiepileptics','Follow up'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**This patient has two separate problems with two separate causes, and the key treats each on its own terms.** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` splits management along exactly this line: **provoked** seizures — febrile convulsions are the textbook example of an acute symptomatic event — are managed by treating the trigger, not by starting a chronic antiepileptic drug (*"PROVOKED: No Chronic AED Treatment. Treat the CAUSE"*, with fever explicitly listed among the causes to treat: *"Treat the CAUSE: ... Fever/infection"*). **Unprovoked** seizures, once recurrent, cross the treatment threshold on the same slide: *"2nd Unprovoked → START AED"*. Three afebrile seizures is recurrent unprovoked seizure activity by that rule and meets the definition of epilepsy (`npqb-nr-161`: *"At least 2 unprovoked seizures occurring >24 hours apart"*), so it earns an antiepileptic; the febrile convulsions earn an antipyretic to control the trigger, not a second drug aimed at the seizure itself.\n\n**Why the febrile episodes stay untreated by an AED even though they recur.** The deck applies the same provoked-cause logic elsewhere to a different trigger — *"Neonatal seizures are PROVOKED until proven otherwise! Find the CAUSE → Treat the cause"* — and the principle generalises: however many times a *provoked* seizure recurs, each recurrence is evidence for treating the trigger better (here, tighter fever and infection control), not for adding a chronic AED that does nothing to a fever-driven event.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:** sodium valproate alone (option a) treats only the epilepsy component and leaves the febrile-convulsion trigger unaddressed — a recurrence during the next fever would not be prevented by it. Two antiepileptics (option c) over-treats the febrile convulsions, which are provoked and do not by themselves warrant any chronic AED regardless of drug count. Follow-up alone (option d) ignores that the afebrile seizures have already crossed the recurrent-unprovoked threshold that this same framework uses to start treatment — this is not a watch-and-wait picture once that threshold is met.\n\n**Bottom line:** provoked and unprovoked seizures in the same patient are treated separately — antipyretic (and cause-directed care) for the fever-driven convulsions, antiepileptic for the recurrent unprovoked ones — and the two are never merged into a single prescription decision.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Febrile (provoked) convulsions are managed by treating the trigger with an antipyretic, never a chronic AED; recurrent afebrile (unprovoked) seizures meet the treatment threshold for an antiepileptic — the two are never merged into one prescription.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.33 (answer p.36)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-165', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'A patient is admitted to the ICU with recurrent seizures and hyponatremia. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Symptomatic seizures','Symptomatic epilepsy','Idiopathic epilepsy'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Hyponatraemia is an identifiable acute metabolic insult, which makes these symptomatic (provoked) seizures rather than epilepsy at all.** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` names the exact mechanism: under acute symptomatic causes it lists *"METABOLIC: Hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, hypocalcemia"*, defines provoked seizures as occurring *"in close temporal relationship with systemic insult (fever, metabolic shift, trauma). These do not constitute epilepsy,"* and repeats hyponatraemia by name in the treatment framework\'s provoked column: *"Treat the CAUSE: ... Hyponatremia."*\n\n**Why a sodium disturbance triggers seizures.** *(This mechanism is not taken from the course material.)* A rapid or severe fall in serum sodium drops extracellular osmolality relative to the intracellular compartment, drawing free water into neurons and glia; the resulting cerebral oedema and altered neuronal membrane excitability lower the seizure threshold generally, without any structural epileptogenic lesion — which is exactly why the fix is to correct the sodium, not to start a chronic antiepileptic, the same "treat the cause" principle applied to fever in `npqb-nr-164`.\n\n**Why the others are wrong.** "Symptomatic epilepsy" (option b) is a category error: epilepsy requires an *enduring* predisposition to seizures, and a metabolic derangement such as hyponatraemia is, by the deck\'s own wording, an acute cause that "does not constitute epilepsy" once corrected — it is symptomatic of the metabolic disturbance, not of an epilepsy syndrome. "Idiopathic epilepsy" (option c) requires the opposite finding — *"No underlying structural cause found"* — which is contradicted here by the presence of a clear metabolic trigger.\n\n**⚠️ This entry opens no back-reference chain.** Nothing later in this topic says "previous case" about it, and it does not itself refer back to an earlier question — it is read and reported here as a standalone entry, not the antecedent the drafting brief predicted for Q168/Q171 (see the file header).\n\n**Bottom line:** recurrent seizures with an identified acute metabolic cause are symptomatic (provoked) seizures, not epilepsy of any kind, and the correct management target is the sodium, not an antiepileptic drug.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recurrent seizures with an identifiable acute metabolic trigger such as hyponatraemia are symptomatic (provoked) seizures, not epilepsy — provoked events do not meet the epilepsy definition even when recurrent.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.34 (answer p.36). ⚠️ Prints only three options (a-c); held as printed.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-166', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'Male child aged 6 years with recent scholastic achievement came to your clinic. His mother told you that he sometimes experiences attacks of lapse of consciousness that last for few seconds with eye blinking. During the attack he would not respond to her but after it, he could. What is the most appropriate investigation to ask for?',
  options:['MRI brain','CT brain','EEG','Nerve conduction study'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Brief staring spells with eye blinking, unresponsiveness during the attack and a clean return to baseline afterwards is the textbook description of absence (petit mal) seizures, and EEG is the confirmatory test.** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` works an almost identical worked case (Mariam, 7 years old): *"Staring spells 50-100x/day. Duration: 5-10 seconds each. Eyes roll up briefly. No post-ictal confusion. EEG at clinic: 3 Hz spike-wave. MRI: Normal"*, diagnosed as *"Childhood Absence Epilepsy (CAE)"* — *"GENERALIZED: Absence (Petit Mal): Sudden stare, 5-30 sec. No postictal phase. 3Hz spike-wave on EEG. Children."* EEG is what demonstrates the diagnostic 3 Hz spike-and-wave discharge, and it is the deck\'s own teaching that at this stage of diagnosis EEG is what supports the impression the history has already built: *"Diagnosis is clinical. EEG & MRI SUPPORT — they don\'t replace your history."*\n\n**Why "recent scholastic achievement" is a clue, not filler.** Absence seizures are frequent and brief enough — up to 50-100 times a day in the deck\'s worked case — to interrupt attention repeatedly without the child, teachers, or even parents noticing anything beyond inattentiveness; a normal-cognition child with new academic difficulty, discovered incidentally, is a classic route to this diagnosis, rather than an obvious dramatic seizure bringing the family in *(the framing of the scholastic clue is not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Why the others are wrong.** MRI and CT brain look for a structural lesion, and the worked case this vignette mirrors is explicitly idiopathic with a normal MRI — imaging is not what confirms the diagnosis here, and is only reached for if red flags (focal signs, an atypical course) appear. A nerve conduction study assesses peripheral nerve function and has no bearing on a generalized seizure disorder *(this exclusion is not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**⚠️ A repair recorded here.** The bank prints options b-d with a duplicated letter baked into the text itself — "b.CT brain", "c.EEG", "d.Nerve conduction study" — a transcription artefact of the printed page, not genuine option text. It is repaired to plain option names in this draft; the printed form is recorded in `source`.\n\n**Bottom line:** brief staring spells with eye blinking and a clean return to baseline in a school-age child are absence seizures, and EEG (looking for the 3 Hz spike-wave pattern) is the investigation of choice — not imaging, and not a peripheral nerve study.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Brief staring spells with eye blinking and a clean return to baseline in a school-age child are absence seizures; EEG showing the 3 Hz spike-and-wave pattern is the confirmatory investigation, not imaging.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.34 (answer p.36). The bank prints options b-d with a duplicated letter baked into the option text itself ("b.CT brain", "c.EEG", "d.Nerve conduction study"); repaired to plain option names here.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-167', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'In previous case, what is the drug of choice for him?',
  options:['Carbamazepine','Valproate','Phenytoin','Low dose Aspirin'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**"Previous case" is `npqb-nr-166`\'s 6-year-old boy with brief staring-and-blinking spells, diagnosed there with absence seizures, and of the four drugs offered only valproate treats absence seizures at all.** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` lists absence-seizure treatment as *"1st Line: Ethosuximide (first choice), Valproate"* — ethosuximide is not on this option list, which makes valproate the best drug actually offered rather than the deck\'s single top choice.\n\n**Why valproate, and not phenytoin, works for a generalized spike-wave disorder.** *(This mechanism is not taken from the course material.)* Valproate is broad-spectrum and dampens the same generalized thalamocortical circuit that drives the 3 Hz spike-and-wave rhythm of absence seizures, whereas carbamazepine and phenytoin act mainly on sodium channels in a way suited to *focal* seizures but, as already established for absence seizures in `npqb-nr-163`, can paradoxically worsen a generalized spike-wave discharge rather than suppress it.\n\n**Why the others are wrong.** Carbamazepine and phenytoin are the two drugs the same slide names explicitly as forbidden in absence seizures — *"NEVER: Carbamazepine"*, *"NEVER: Phenytoin"* — repeated in the deck\'s worked case as *"NEVER use Carbamazepine or Phenytoin in absence seizures — they worsen seizures!"* (the same principle tested directly in `npqb-nr-163`). Low-dose aspirin has no antiepileptic role of any kind *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Bottom line:** valproate is the drug of choice offered here for absence seizures because it acts on the generalized circuit driving them, while carbamazepine and phenytoin are actively contraindicated because they target the wrong mechanism for this diagnosis.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'For the absence-seizure boy of the previous case, valproate is the appropriate drug on this menu; carbamazepine and phenytoin are both contraindicated in absence seizures.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.34 (answer p.36)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-168', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'In previous case, what is the most probable cause of illness for this child?',
  options:['Symptomatic to brain tumor','Symptomatic to encephalitis','Idiopathic'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Again the "previous case" is `npqb-nr-166`\'s 6-year-old boy with brief staring-and-blinking spells, and this question asks a genuinely different thing from either earlier question in the chain — not the diagnosis (`npqb-nr-166`) or the drug (`npqb-nr-167`, valproate), but the cause.** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` gives the matching worked case its aetiology directly: *"IDIOPATHIC (Genetic): Childhood onset. Normal MRI. Family Hx common"*, and childhood absence epilepsy is the deck\'s prototype of an idiopathic generalized epilepsy — *"IDIOPATHIC (Genetic): No underlying structural cause found"*, a category the deck\'s own causes chart labels **40%** beside the idiopathic segment, opposite the several symptomatic/structural categories on the other side of the same chart.\n\n**Why the others are wrong.** Brain tumour and encephalitis are both listed on the same chart as **symptomatic/structural** causes of epilepsy (*"Neoplastic: Primary/metastatic brain tumors"*; *"Infectious: Meningitis, encephalitis"*), but nothing in the vignette supports either. A structural or infective cause would be expected to add red-flag features this history lacks — fever or systemic illness for encephalitis, and focal neurological signs, a progressive course, or altered baseline behaviour for a tumour *(the specific red-flag list is not taken from the course material; the two causal categories themselves are)*. A clean, stereotyped absence-seizure picture in a school-age child, without any of those, points to the idiopathic (genetic) category instead — the same profile as the deck\'s own Mariam case, whose family history of similar episodes reinforced the idiopathic label.\n\n**Bottom line:** childhood absence epilepsy of this classic, uncomplicated kind is idiopathic (genetic), not symptomatic to a structural or infective lesion — the absence of red flags is doing as much diagnostic work here as the seizure itself.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A classic, uncomplicated childhood absence-seizure presentation with no red-flag features is idiopathic (genetic) in cause, not symptomatic to a tumour or encephalitis.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.34 (answer p.36)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-169', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'Male patient with partial complex seizures that started at the age of 43 years, what is the first investigation to ask for?',
  options:['Functional brain imaging','MRI Brain','EEG','CSF examination'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The bank prints an explanation box for this question, transcribed here verbatim:**\n\n> partial complex seizures that begin in mid-life (e at age 43 in this case) are typically considered new-onset seizures and warrant a thorough investigation to determine the underlying cause.\n\nComplex partial seizures are the modern *focal, impaired-awareness* seizure — `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy`: *"Focal IMPAIRED Awareness: Consciousness is altered. May stare, automatisms. No recall of event. Most common type in TLE"* — and the deck names structural imaging as the tool for finding a cause once a seizure is established clinically: *"MRI Brain: Detects structural causes; protocol matters — epilepsy sequences essential."*\n\n**Why age of onset changes the priority from classification to cause-hunting.** The idiopathic (genetic) epilepsies in this deck are framed around childhood and adolescent onset — Mariam\'s absence epilepsy at 7, Yasin\'s JME at 16 — while the causes chart lists vascular and neoplastic causes (stroke, haemorrhage, primary or metastatic brain tumours) among the symptomatic/structural category, both of which rise in incidence with age. A first focal seizure at 43, with no prior seizure history, is far more likely to be symptomatic of a new structural lesion than to represent a late-onset idiopathic syndrome, which is what makes finding the cause — not just classifying the seizure — the urgent step.\n\n**Why the others are wrong.** EEG (option c) is genuinely part of the work-up and confirms epileptiform activity, but the deck is explicit that *"Diagnosis is clinical. EEG & MRI SUPPORT — they don\'t replace your history"* — and a new-onset focal seizure at 43 raises the question of *what caused it*, which is an imaging question, not primarily a classification one. **Functional brain imaging** (PET/SPECT) belongs to the pre-surgical work-up of drug-resistant focal epilepsy, not the first test at new onset *(not taken from the course material)*. **CSF examination** is reserved for suspected infective or inflammatory causes and is not the routine first step for an otherwise unexplained new-onset seizure.\n\n**Bottom line:** a new-onset focal (complex partial) seizure in mid-life is investigated first with structural MRI, because age alone shifts the likely cause toward a structural lesion that the classification-only tests (EEG) cannot find.',
  objective:'New-onset complex partial seizures beginning in mid-life warrant structural MRI first, to identify an underlying cause; EEG classifies, it does not replace the search for a lesion.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.34 (answer p.36, explanation box printed)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-170', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'12-year-old female admitted to ER. Her father reports that she had convulsions for 40 minutes, but there was no tongue biting and no falling. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Epilepsy','Pseudoseizures','Focal seizures','Generalized tonic-clonic seizures'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The bank prints an explanation box for this question, transcribed here verbatim:**\n\n> The description of no tongue biting and no falling during the 40-minute convulsion is atypical for generalized tonic-clonic seizures.\n\nBoth features the box calls atypical are named directly in `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy`\'s own differentiating table for psychogenic non-epileptic seizures (PNES): duration is *"Often Prolonged (>5 minutes)"* against a true seizure\'s *"Short (<2-3 minutes)"*, and self-injury/incontinence is *"No"* for PNES against *"Yes"* for a true epileptic seizure. Forty minutes is not merely longer than typical — it is roughly eight times the upper bound the deck gives for a genuine tonic-clonic seizure, and it sits well inside the PNES range; combined with the absence of a fall or tongue injury, the vignette lines up with several rows of the same table at once, not just one.\n\n**Why the others are wrong.** "Epilepsy" (option a) and "generalized tonic-clonic seizures" (option d) both predict the features the box says are missing — a real GTCS would be expected to include a fall (tonic phase) and carries a real risk of lateral tongue biting, plus cyanosis and post-ictal confusion this vignette does not mention. "Focal seizures" (option c) does not fit either: the stem describes a prolonged, generalized-looking convulsive event, not a discrete focal attack, and focal seizures typically last one to a few minutes rather than forty.\n\n**Bottom line:** a prolonged (40-minute) convulsive episode with no fall and no tongue biting is atypical for a true generalized tonic-clonic seizure on duration alone, and the missing injury features confirm it — together they point to pseudoseizures (PNES) instead.',
  objective:'A prolonged convulsive episode lacking a fall or tongue biting is atypical for a true generalized tonic-clonic seizure and points instead to pseudoseizures (PNES).',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.34 (answer p.36, explanation box printed)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-171', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'In previous case, what is the most useful diagnostic tool during the episode?',
  options:['MRI Brain','Blood work','Video EEG','CT Scan'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**"Previous case" is the 40-minute convulsion of `npqb-nr-170`, kept from her own diagnosis of pseudoseizures, and this question asks for the tool that confirms it, not the diagnosis itself.** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` names it directly, twice: in the PNES differentiation table, *"Video-EEG Monitoring remains the gold standard for definitive differentiation"*, and again in the diagnostic-tools list, *"Video-EEG Monitoring: Gold standard: captures clinical + electrical simultaneously"*.\n\n**Why video-EEG beats a routine (non-video) EEG for exactly this question.** A routine EEG records only the electrical trace, often between episodes, and an interictal EEG can be normal in genuine epilepsy just as easily as in PNES — the deck notes plain EEG *"may be normal between seizures."* What actually settles the diagnosis is capturing an episode itself and reading the trace *during* it: if the patient\'s typical clinical event occurs with a flat, non-epileptiform trace, that is the definitive finding for PNES; if the trace shows epileptiform discharges timed to the clinical event, that instead confirms a true seizure. A routine EEG cannot make this pairing because it does not record the clinical behaviour alongside the electrical signal.\n\n**Why the others are wrong.** MRI Brain and CT Scan are static structural studies; they show anatomy, not what is happening *during* an episode, and pseudoseizures are typically structurally normal in any case. Blood work has no established role in confirming PNES during the event itself *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Bottom line:** video-EEG, which records the clinical event and the EEG trace simultaneously, is the gold-standard tool for confirming pseudoseizures during an episode — it is the pairing of behaviour and trace, not either alone, that a routine EEG cannot supply.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Video-EEG monitoring, capturing the clinical event and the EEG simultaneously, is the gold-standard diagnostic tool for confirming pseudoseizures during an episode; structural imaging shows nothing about the event itself.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.34 (answer p.36). The bank prints the stem as "IN previous case"; corrected to *In* here.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-172', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'In previous case, what is the appropriate treatment for this condition?',
  options:['Antiepileptic drugs','Psychotherapy','Surgery','Hospitalization for observation'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Still the same "previous case" — the 40-minute convulsion diagnosed there as pseudoseizures in `npqb-nr-170` — and this closes the chain by asking for management, distinct from `npqb-nr-170`\'s diagnosis and `npqb-nr-171`\'s diagnostic tool.** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` describes pseudoseizures (PNES) as a **functional** disorder — *"Functional Disorder: Often linked to psychological distress, trauma, or internal conflicts"* — and frames it as *"a functional neurology disorder linked to psychological distress, trauma, and life stress ... Needs compassionate, non-judgmental care"*, which points management toward a psychological approach rather than a pharmacological or surgical one.\n\n**Why treating it as epilepsy is actively harmful, not just ineffective.** An AED gives the patient the side-effect burden of an antiepileptic (sedation, teratogenicity for some agents, monitoring requirements) with no seizure to treat, while the real driver — the underlying psychological distress the deck names — goes unaddressed and the episodes continue. Getting the diagnosis right via video-EEG (`npqb-nr-171`) is what makes it possible to redirect care toward psychotherapy instead of accumulating drug exposure for a condition drugs cannot touch.\n\n**Why the others are wrong.** Antiepileptic drugs treat the abnormal electrical discharges of true epilepsy; PNES is explicitly *"not caused by abnormal electrical discharges in the brain,"* so an AED has no mechanism to act on. Surgery has no role in a functional disorder — there is no epileptogenic focus to resect. Hospitalisation for observation is a diagnostic step (which is how video-EEG capture, `npqb-nr-171`, is often obtained), not itself a treatment.\n\n**Bottom line:** pseudoseizures are managed with psychotherapy addressing the underlying psychological distress, not with antiepileptic drugs, surgery, or observation alone — and treating them as epilepsy adds drug risk without touching the actual cause.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Pseudoseizures (PNES) are treated with psychotherapy directed at the underlying psychological distress; antiepileptic drugs and surgery have no mechanism to act on a functional disorder.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.34 (answer p.36)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-173', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'A patient has had 4 recurrent seizures in the last hour, with the patient regaining consciousness between each seizure. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Serial epileptic fits','Symptomatic seizure','Status epilepticus','Psychogenic non-epileptic seizures'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Regaining consciousness between attacks is the exact feature that rules out status epilepticus, which is why the key is serial (cluster) fits instead.** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` defines status epilepticus by the absence of recovery: *"DEFINITION: Seizure ≥5 min OR ≥2 seizures without recovery → ≥30 min = permanent neuronal injury risk"* — the defining word is *without* recovery between events. This patient recovers consciousness after each of the four seizures, which places the picture in the milder category of repeated (serial) seizures with inter-ictal recovery, not status epilepticus.\n\n**Why the distinction is not academic.** The deck\'s status-epilepticus protocol escalates specifically because a brain deprived of recovery between seizures accumulates injury risk once the total time without recovery passes roughly 30 minutes: *"ABC → Diazepam (5-10 min) → Phenytoin (10-30 min) → Refractory (>30 min): ICU, IV thiopental/propofol/midazolam."* A patient who recovers consciousness between fits has not crossed that threshold and does not need the full emergency ladder, but four seizures inside one hour is still a warning that the patient is trending toward status if the pattern continues — it is managed urgently, just not by the same refractory-status protocol, and it should prompt looking for a precipitant the way any cluster of seizures does.\n\n**Why the others are wrong.** "Symptomatic seizure" (option b) describes an aetiology (an identifiable provoking cause), not this clinical pattern, and nothing in the stem names a provoking insult — the question is about the *pattern* of the seizures, which "symptomatic" does not address at all. "Status epilepticus" (option c) is exactly what the recovery of consciousness between seizures excludes, by the deck\'s own definition. "Psychogenic non-epileptic seizures" (option d) is not supported — nothing in the vignette describes the atypical semiology (prolonged duration, no injury, side-to-side head shaking) that would suggest PNES; four seizures with recovery of consciousness between each is the description of a genuine seizure cluster, not a functional event.\n\n**Bottom line:** the presence or absence of recovered consciousness *between* seizures is what separates serial epileptic fits from status epilepticus — recovery between them means it is not status, even though the cluster still warrants urgent evaluation before it can evolve into one.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recovery of consciousness between repeated seizures rules out status epilepticus and defines serial (cluster) epileptic fits instead — the presence or absence of inter-ictal recovery is the deciding feature.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.34 (answer p.36). The bank prints the stem without its leading article ("patient has had 4 recurrent seizures"); restored to *A patient has had* here.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-174', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'What is the EEG finding in the case of psychogenic pseudoseizures?',
  options:['Always negative','Negative but can be positive','Positive with epileptiform activity','Abnormal with generalized slowing'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The EEG in pseudoseizures is characteristically negative during the event, but the bank keys the guarded "negative but can be positive" over the absolute "always negative".** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy`\'s own differentiation tables state the expected finding plainly — *"EEG during event: NORMAL"* for the PNES case, and *"EEG: No EEG changes"* for pseudoseizure against *"Epileptiform changes"* for a true seizure in the general comparison table.\n\n**Why "always" is too strong, and the others are wrong.** A patient can have genuine epilepsy *and* PNES together — a recognised overlap in clinical practice — or carry an incidental interictal epileptiform abnormality unrelated to the functional event itself, so a small number of pseudoseizure EEGs are not perfectly clean *(this nuance beyond the deck\'s simplified table is not taken from the course material)*. This is also why the vignette in this topic that turns on PNES (`npqb-nr-170` through `npqb-nr-172`) reaches for **video-EEG** rather than a bare EEG report before committing to the diagnosis — a single "positive" or "negative" EEG in isolation cannot fully settle the question, the same reasoning this option is testing in the abstract. "Positive with epileptiform activity" (option c) and "abnormal with generalized slowing" (option d) both describe patterns expected in genuine seizure disorders, not in a functional event with no underlying electrical discharge.\n\n**Bottom line:** the EEG in pseudoseizures is negative in the great majority of cases, but "always" overstates it — a small number can still show an unrelated or coexisting abnormality, which is exactly why video-EEG rather than a snapshot EEG is the tool the rest of this topic relies on.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The EEG during a pseudoseizure is typically negative because there is no underlying epileptiform discharge, but "always negative" overstates it; epileptiform activity or generalized slowing instead points to a genuine seizure disorder.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.34 (answer p.36)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-175', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'Which of the following anti-seizures drugs works best for a patient with juvenile Myoclonic epilepsy?',
  options:['Ethosuximide','Phenytoin','Carbamazepine','Phenobarbitone','Sodium valproate'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**Sodium valproate is first-line for juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME) on both the general myoclonic-seizure row and the deck\'s own worked JME case, and two of the remaining options are explicitly forbidden.** `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy`, general table: *"MYOCLONIC SEIZURES: 1st Line: Valproate (Depakote)"*. The deck\'s worked case (Yasin, 16, morning myoclonic jerks with a recent generalized tonic-clonic seizure, polyspike-wave EEG) is diagnosed as JME and treated: *"1st Line: Valproate 15-25 mg/kg/day. 2nd Line: Levetiracetam. NEVER: Carbamazepine or Phenytoin!"* — the same two drugs already established as contraindicated in absence seizures (`npqb-nr-163`), for the same underlying reason: a sodium-channel-directed mechanism that can worsen a generalized epilepsy rather than treat it.\n\n**Why the choice does not stop at "first-line" in real practice.** The same worked case flags that valproate is teratogenic, so a patient of reproductive age needs contraception alongside it — *"Women: Contraception needed (VPA teratogenic)"* — with levetiracetam named as the fallback where that is a concern. JME is also lifelong in most patients and needs sleep hygiene alongside any drug, since sleep deprivation is a recognised trigger for the morning jerks.\n\n**Why the others are wrong.** **Carbamazepine** and **phenytoin** are the two drugs the same case labels *"NEVER"* for JME — they can worsen myoclonic and generalized seizures. **Ethosuximide** is the first-line drug for pure **absence** seizures, not myoclonic epilepsy, and does not cover the generalized tonic-clonic component that JME patients often also have. **Phenobarbitone** is an older broad-spectrum antiepileptic with no place named on this slide\'s JME or myoclonic-seizure treatment rows *(this specific exclusion is not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Bottom line:** sodium valproate is first-line for JME; carbamazepine and phenytoin are contraindicated because they worsen generalized seizures by the wrong mechanism, and ethosuximide belongs to absence seizures rather than JME.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Sodium valproate is first-line for juvenile myoclonic epilepsy; carbamazepine and phenytoin are contraindicated because they can worsen it, and ethosuximide belongs to absence seizures instead.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.35 (answer p.36)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-176', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-epilepsy',
  stem:'What instruction should be given to the public about care of the patient during an attack of generalized tonic clonic seizure?',
  options:['Protect the body by mechanical restraint to avoid self-injury','Give him a sip of water to get him alert','Let the patient in the standing position','Do not put anything in his mouth'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**"Do not put anything in his mouth" is the standard seizure first-aid instruction; every other option is a recognised way bystanders make a convulsion worse.** *(The specific first-aid list below is not taken from the course material — the cached lecture covers hospital status-epilepticus protocol, not lay first aid; the principles are standard and widely taught.)*\n\nDuring a generalized tonic-clonic seizure, consciousness is lost and the jaw and airway reflexes cannot be relied on. Putting anything in the mouth — a spoon, a cloth, fingers, or a sip of water — risks dental or airway injury, aspiration, or choking, and does not prevent tongue-biting the way it is popularly believed to. The correct actions are to protect the patient from injury by clearing the surrounding area and cushioning the head, turn the patient onto their side to keep the airway clear, time the seizure, and never restrain the movements.\n\n**Why the folk belief persists.** The instinct to protect the mouth comes from the popular myth that a seizing patient can "swallow their tongue" — anatomically implausible, since the tongue is anchored in the floor of the mouth by its own muscles and cannot slip back to block the airway on its own *(this anatomical point is not taken from the course material)*. What genuinely protects the airway is the lateral (recovery) position, which lets saliva and any vomitus drain away rather than pool at the back of the throat — a mechanical fix, not the object-in-the-mouth intervention the myth calls for.\n\n**Why the others are wrong.** **Mechanical restraint** (option a) can itself cause fractures or dislocations against the forceful tonic-clonic muscle contractions, and does not stop the seizure. **A sip of water** (option b) is dangerous precisely because it puts something in the mouth of an unconscious patient with unprotected airway reflexes — the same hazard the correct answer warns against, offered here as its own distractor. **Standing the patient up** (option c) risks a fall and injury during a convulsion in which postural control is already lost; the safe position is lying on the side, not upright.\n\n**Bottom line:** during a generalized tonic-clonic seizure, protect from injury and turn to the side — never restrain, never give anything by mouth, and never try to stand the patient up.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Seizure first aid is protection from injury and the lateral (recovery) position — never restrain the patient, put anything in the mouth, or attempt to stand them up.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.35 (answer p.36)'
}
